|re2c| can accelerate hot greedy loops in regular expressions using SIMD
hardware vectorization in C (SSE2 / AVX2 intrinsics) and Go 1.27+
(``simd/archsimd``).

In lexical analysis, a significant percentage of CPU cycles is spent
consuming long homogeneous byte sequences (such as string literals,
identifiers, URL fields, or whitespace).

Instead of processing these loops byte-by-byte with scalar branch dispatches,
the vectorization pass emits a fast-forward loop that loads and evaluates
blocks of 16 or 32 bytes simultaneously using parallel range checks and bitmask
extraction, then hands off the first byte outside the class to the ordinary
scalar DFA dispatcher.

When enabled with ``--arch x86 --vectorize-loops`` (or ``re2c:arch = x86;
re2c:vectorize:loops = 1;``):

- Vectorization requires selecting a supported target architecture with
  ``--arch <arch>`` / ``re2c:arch = <arch>`` (currently ``x86`` for the C and
  Go backends). The vector width in bytes is controlled by
  ``--vectorize-size <16 | 32>`` / ``re2c:vectorize:size`` (``32`` by default:
  AVX2 ``__m256i`` / ``archsimd.Int8x32``; ``16`` selects SSE2 ``__m128i`` /
  ``archsimd.Int8x16``).
- Vectorization supports all code models (``--goto-label``, ``--loop-switch`` and
  ``--recursive-functions``), all APIs (``simple``, ``record`` and ``generic``),
  ``--eager-skip`` and ``--storable-state`` (``-f``). It requires single-byte code
  units (``YYCTYPE`` must be an unsigned 1-byte code unit type), ``YYFILL`` with
  bounds checking, and no end-of-input rule (``re2c:eof``) or ``--skeleton``. Any
  unsupported combination is a configuration error, not a silent fallback. With
  ``--emit-dot`` the option is reset.
- The compiler selects base states with a nonzero refill requirement and a
  reflexive character class of at most 8 disjoint ranges. The loop must not record
  backtracking positions, perform tag actions or have end-of-input transitions,
  the loop entry state must not have a hoisted cursor increment, and neither the
  loop state nor its body may carry final, fallback or end-of-input tags. Tags
  elsewhere in the DFA do not block the loop: they are set and read by scalar code
  at positions the loop never skips.
- Both ``re2c:yyfill:enable`` and ``re2c:yyfill:check`` must be enabled to ensure
  the vector loop stops before the end-of-buffer boundary. It reserves enough
  lookahead for the scalar dispatcher via ``code:yylessthan`` and does not
  increase ``YYMAXFILL``.
- With ``--api generic``, vector loads use the width-specific primitives
  ``re2c:YYPEEK16`` (for width 16) and ``re2c:YYPEEK32`` (for width 32), cursor
  advancement uses ``YYSHIFT``, bounds checks use ``YYLESSTHAN``, and reloading
  ``yych`` uses ``YYPEEK``. No ``YYCURSOR``, ``YYLIMIT`` or ``YYINPUT`` identifier
  is emitted in generic API output.
- The vector block is emitted on the reflexive transition of the loop state rather
  than at state entry. Control flow is emitted entirely through |re2c|'s existing
  IR constructs (``code:if_then_else``, ``code:loop``, ``code:var_local``,
  ``code:assign``, ``code:yyshift`` and ``code:yypeek``), combined with atomic
  target expression templates (``code:type_vec{16,32}``,
  ``code:type_vec{16,32}_mask``, ``code:yypeek{16,32}``, ``code:vec{16,32}_splat``,
  ``code:vec{16,32}_{sub,cmpeq,cmpgt,or}``, ``code:vec{16,32}_{movemask,ctz}``
  and ``code:vec{16,32}_cleanup``).
- Exit ranges in the complement of the reflexive class are tested with equality
  (``cmpeq``) for singletons and signed greater-than (``cmpgt``) with a
  precomputed ``+128`` wrapping bias for byte intervals (a single ``cmpgt``
  without ``sub`` when an exit range ends at ``0x7F`` or starts at ``0x80``),
  avoiding the extra zero register required by saturating-subtraction range
  tests.
- In C, the generated code uses ``<immintrin.h>`` (compile with ``-mavx2`` for
  width 32 or ``-msse2`` for width 16) and ``__builtin_ctz`` (GCC / Clang). No
  explicit ``_mm256_zeroupper()`` is emitted in C: GCC at ``-O2`` and above and
  Clang insert ``vzeroupper`` automatically; when compiling 32-byte AVX2 lexers
  with GCC below ``-O2`` (e.g. ``-O0``, ``-O1`` or ``-Os``) alongside legacy
  non-VEX SSE code, pass ``-mvzeroupper`` explicitly.
- In Go, the generated code requires Go 1.27+ with ``GOEXPERIMENT=simd`` and
  imports ``math/bits`` and ``simd/archsimd``. For width 32 (YMM), the Go
  template emits ``archsimd.ClearAVXUpperBits()`` on normal loop exit; for
  width 16 (XMM), no cleanup call is emitted. The caller must maintain
  ``0 <= YYCURSOR <= YYLIMIT <= len(YYINPUT)`` and a sound length guard.
- The default C vector output mixes declarations and statements inside blocks and
  therefore requires C99 or later (or C++). Generated lexers do not check CPU
  capabilities at run time.

When vectorization pays off
---------------------------

The vector block has a fixed entry cost, paid once per run of the class that
is at least two code units long, and a per-block cost that is small compared
to one scalar step per code unit. Vectorization therefore wins only when runs
are longer than a break-even length (near 9 code units on x86-64 C), and loses
below it. On corpora where most runs are short (identifiers, numbers, short
words), the vectorized lexer is slower than the scalar one; on corpora with
long runs (string bodies, whitespace, comments, URL and log fields) it is
faster. Enable it per block for grammars whose hot loops scan long runs, and
verify on representative input.

Go string input
---------------

In Go ``simple`` and ``record`` APIs, ``archsimd.LoadUint8x16`` /
``LoadUint8x32`` require ``YYINPUT`` to be a byte slice rather than a
``string``. For string input, construct a read-only byte view once before
entering the lexer via ``unsafe.StringData`` and ``unsafe.Slice``:

.. code-block:: go

    func lex(text string) int {
        input := unsafe.Slice(unsafe.StringData(text), len(text))
        yycursor, yylimit := 0, len(input)
        /*!re2c
            re2c:arch = x86;
            re2c:vectorize:loops = 1;
            re2c:api = simple;
            re2c:YYCTYPE = byte;
            re2c:YYINPUT = input;
            re2c:YYFILL = "return -2";
            re2c:YYFILL:naked = 1;
            [a-z]+ { return yycursor }
            *      { return -1 }
        */
    }

Do not put ``[]byte(text[...])`` inside the lexer: Go may copy every block and
call into the runtime with dirty YMM registers.

In Go, the variable cursor advancement by ``yyn`` on a vector match is of type
``int`` (``yycursor += yyn``). For a cursor of another type, such as ``uint``,
set ``re2c:yyshift:conversion = 1;`` and ``re2c:YYPTYPE = <type>;`` so that the
shift is converted explicitly (``yycursor += uint(yyn)``).
